  "query_preview": "..\nGusty winds in the wake of this mornings cold front will continue \ninto the afternoon hours before returning to much calmer conditons \nthis evening and overnight. Otherwise, a mild and unsettled \nweek is expected as a progressive weather pattern continues \nacross the region bringing multiple waves of precipitation \nchances, and increasing snow levels. \n\nThe main concerns this week: The main concerns will be for \ndebris flows/rockslides, particularly in and around burn scars \nin the Cascades due to snow melt and rain. Chances of 24 hour \nrain amounts exceeding an inch near the Cascade crest are are up\nto 40-60+% (NBM, even higher in REFS) Tuesday, and includes up \nto 45% chance in the western portion of the Little Giant burn \nscar. Similar probabilities again for the weekend. As discussed \nin previous forecasts, most rivers should behave, however the \nStehekin River at Stehekin is showing a 10% chance of reaching \nMinor Flood Stage (20.5 ft) on Wednesday. These probabilities \ndecrease briefly for Thursday/ Friday and return with a 10% of \nexceeding Minor Flood Stage on Saturday.\n\nWhen to expect precipitation: The next round of precipitation \nis expected to pick up tomorrow evening across the area. Snow \nlevels will be 5000ft + near the Canadian border and 7000ft over\nSE WA into the Central Panhandle Mountains. Tuesday follows \nwith another round of precipitation primarily across northern WA\nand N ID Panhandle, with snow levels continuing to increase \ninto the 7000-8000+ ft range. \n\nThere is very good agreement across ensembles of a brief period\nof ridging over the region Wednesday and Thursday while a \ntrough digs south just off the west coast. Most ensemble data \npoints to the region being influenced by troughing / southwest \nflow for the weekend as a low pressure system develops off the \ncoast of BC. There are some inconsistencies across ensembles of \nhow strong and east this system is placed by the weekend. \nHowever, expect precipitation to return for the weekend along \nwith breezy winds.",
